      <!-- PART 7: CIPP/E EXAM CHEAT SHEET -->
      <!-- ========================================================================= -->
      <section id="faq-section" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <div class="border-b border-slate-200 pb-4">
          <span class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-red-900">Study Guide</span>
          <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 mt-1">
            CIPP/E High-Yield Exam Cheat Sheet: Cross-Border Transfers
          </h2>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-6 space-y-6">
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-6 shadow-sm">
            <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-900">
              Q: Can a company rely on Article 49 Derogations (e.g. Consent) for repetitive corporate transfers?
            </h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
              <strong>Answer: NO.</strong> Under EDPB Guidelines 2/2018, Article 49 derogations (explicit consent, contract performance, vital interests) are <strong>strictly exceptional</strong> and can only be used for occasional, non-repetitive transfers. Relying on user consent to validate the continuous, bulk transfer of user data across the Atlantic is strictly prohibited.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-6 shadow-sm">
            <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-900">
              Q: What does "Essential Equivalence" mean in European transfer law?
            </h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
              <strong>Answer:</strong> Originating in <em>Schrems I</em>, the CJEU established that a third country does not need to duplicate European privacy law word-for-word. However, it must guarantee a level of protection of fundamental rights and freedoms that is <strong>"essentially equivalent"</strong> to that guaranteed within the European Union under the GDPR read in light of the EU Charter of Fundamental Rights.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-6 shadow-sm">
            <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-900">
              Q: Do non-US persons have Fourth Amendment protections in US courts?
            </h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
              <strong>Answer: NO.</strong> The Fourth Amendment to the United States Constitution protects "the people" against unreasonable searches and seizures. Settled US Supreme Court jurisprudence establishes that foreign nationals outside sovereign US territory possess zero Fourth Amendment rights. Consequently, non-US citizens lack legal standing to challenge NSA intelligence collection before federal courts.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-6 shadow-sm">
            <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-900">
              Q: What are Binding Corporate Rules (BCRs)?
            </h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
              <strong>Answer:</strong> Governed by <a href="/gdpr/articles#article-46" class="text-red-900 underline font-semibold">Article 47 GDPR</a>, BCRs are an internal code of conduct defining global privacy policies across an entire multinational corporate group. Unlike SCCs, BCRs require formal review and approval by a lead European Supervisory Authority and the EDPB. However, post-<em>Schrems II</em>, BCRs suffer from the same limitation as SCCs: they are internal corporate rules that cannot override foreign sovereign surveillance statutes.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

